WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.775 --> 00:00:04.860
Get down.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.350
I think people hear "robot"

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:10.770
and they think Terminator or RoboCop.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.833
Your move, creep.

00:00:13.833 --> 00:00:16.320
The idea that you could
build a new organism

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.210
that has never existed from cells

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.150
that does something
we've never seen before,

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:22.470
essentially a biological machine,

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:25.200
has really captured the imagination.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.030
If xenobots sound like science
fiction brought to life,

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:32.750
it's because they are.
They're biological machines

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:36.623
made by humans but designed by computers.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:40.160
This project offers a new way

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:43.000
to make what you might call a robot

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.000
that's very different from
existing robotic technologies.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.860
A machine for a new purpose,
not to recapitulate biology

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:53.900
or build a heart, but to build
something that could do work

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.150
for any number of tasks

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:57.600
that you'd use a traditional robot for.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:58.970
So it's kind of a weird marriage

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:02.653
between robotics and
computer science and biology.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.410
These tiny specks seen here

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:20.230
under a microscope are xenobots,

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:23.870
made of about 5,000 living skin cells.

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:24.703
They get their name

00:01:24.703 --> 00:01:27.470
from the African clawed
frog Xenopus Laevis,

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:29.820
which supplies their cells.

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.590
They can't reproduce
and they will only live

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:35.950
for around 10 days, feeding
on the small platelets

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.340
of yolk that fill each of their cells.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:39.930
Really, this was the
first time we thought,

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:41.270
could you take stem cells

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:43.270
and build something from the ground up?

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:46.730
And the idea is really
to take these stem cells

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:49.840
very early in development, collect them

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.370
and essentially have
different types of cells,

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:54.850
like you would different Lego blocks.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:57.670
Cells already act like little machines,

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:01.840
some contract and expand,
others capture material.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.200
By shaping these cells,
you create the machine.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:08.260
So you could imagine a red
Lego block is a heart cell

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.880
and a green Lego block is a skin cell.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:12.670
And then really similar to building Legos

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:14.930
where you just sort of
start connecting the pieces

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:17.770
from the bottom up, we build
our robots in the same way.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.100
We just build cell by cell

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:21.050
until we get to a final shape and form.

00:02:23.893 --> 00:02:25.310
Xenobots are designed

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:28.210
and then evolve using
artificial intelligence

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:30.350
before they're made.

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.540
Sam Kriegman is a graduate student

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.330
at the University of Vermont

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:36.480
and designed that computer program.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.420
Unlike every other organism

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:42.640
that has ever existed
before on this planet,

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.640
the xenobots evolve inside
of computer simulations

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:51.420
and the computer simulates
the physical world,

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:56.420
but it can do so very rapidly
and many worlds at once

00:02:56.470 --> 00:03:01.470
and so, it can do evolution
in days instead of millennia

00:03:01.498 --> 00:03:03.968
and at the end of this
evolutionary process,

00:03:03.968 --> 00:03:07.630
it prints out a blueprint that tells us

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:11.530
how to build a new organism
for some desired task

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:16.013
from essentially scratch from
existing biological materials.

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:20.550
The simulated xenobots are subjected

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:24.310
to physics engines similar
to those in video games.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:28.320
The AI assesses how variants
will perform and makes changes

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.790
according to the tasks they're set.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:34.160
This is natural selection
put on fast-forward

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:36.383
with a computer at the controls.

00:03:37.974 --> 00:03:40.632
Evolution on Earth works as
a long chain of mutations,

00:03:40.632 --> 00:03:43.800
Evolution on Earth works as
a long chain of mutations,

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.890
tiny revisions to existing
designs, one after the other

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:50.450
over millions or billions of years.

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:52.530
And sometimes, we can speed this up

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:56.940
by selectively breeding
crops and livestock and dogs

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:00.440
so that their offspring have
desired characteristics.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:01.610
But it's very unlikely

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:04.350
that you could select from wolves or dogs

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:07.080
anything that you wouldn't call a dog.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:11.490
But with computers, we
can speed up this process.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:14.970
So we can simulate a
billion years of evolution

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:17.593
in a day or in a week on a super computer.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:22.130
The computer selects its
top candidates to build

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:25.800
and only then do humans
come back into the picture.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:29.140
The AI generated blueprints
are sent off to Dr. Blackiston

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:32.713
and his wet lab at Tufts
University, Massachusetts.

00:04:40.814 --> 00:04:41.730
Right, so this

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:44.280
is the fun part, it's a bit like sculpting

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:46.290
and some designs are not possible

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:48.390
to be built biologically currently.

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:51.130
They have features that
are too difficult to shape

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:54.520
or construct like right
angles, very small gaps.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:57.210
But we often get five
or six excellent designs

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:59.040
that are completely buildable in the lab,

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.730
which is where we port
everything over into the wet lab

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:03.400
and build them by hand manually.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.310
We then build from the ground up

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:07.700
using those different cells
the shape that new one,

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:09.950
and it typically starts as a sphere.

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:11.500
So the cells round off

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:15.320
and make a roughly round
shape after 24 hours

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:18.090
and then we go in with a
pair of surgical forceps

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:20.900
that we polish with the
sharpening stone manually

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:23.430
and also a very small cautery electorate.

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:26.300
We carve away from that sphere the design

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.260
and sculpt the final shape

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:30.780
that we would like to
match the computer design

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:32.550
and that's really sort of the amazing part

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:35.280
in the innovation is this,
this sculpting aspect

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:38.860
to give you a very, very
precise three-dimensional shape

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:41.363
at only the scale of about
half of a millimeter.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:46.170
Right now, xenobots are made
of only about 5,000 skin

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:50.190
and heart muscle cells, but
they can perform basic tasks

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:51.720
like moving in a straight line,

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:54.380
carrying objects from one spot to another

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:55.813
and behaving collectively.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:00.380
Xenobots don't need to
be that sophisticated

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:03.780
to supply some kind of social utility.

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:06.400
We found that even the xenobots

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.600
that simply swirl around in their dish,

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:13.920
they tended to clean their
dish as they moved through it.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:18.560
So that is, they tended
spontaneously to sweep up

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:22.310
and corral any detritus in their dish

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:25.560
into centralized, neat little piles.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:28.450
Why they do that is still
somewhat of a mystery.

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:32.620
We evolve them in the computer
simulation simply to move

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:36.640
and we got something for free
because they're living systems

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:41.460
that have their own agenda
and do useful things

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:44.403
that weren't necessarily
programmed, like cleaning.

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:47.890
At the moment, all xenobots

00:06:47.890 --> 00:06:50.630
are constructed out of frog cells,

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:54.050
but if human cells were used instead,

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:57.600
then there's huge medical
potential for these machines.

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:00.980
Potentially, they could simply by swirling

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:05.980
around in an artery, scrape
plaque off of the walls

00:07:05.982 --> 00:07:10.232
or abrade calcium deposits
in arthritic joints.

00:07:10.232 --> 00:07:12.464
or abrade calcium deposits
in arthritic joints.

00:07:12.464 --> 00:07:14.650
With a little bit more bio-engineering,

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:18.270
we can imagine targeted drug delivery,

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:20.260
which is something that
we've been thinking about

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:22.670
even in our very simple simulations.

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:27.350
So, when we evolve inside
of a computer a xenobot

00:07:27.350 --> 00:07:29.180
to carry a little pellet

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:32.020
from one position to
another target position,

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:34.960
what that's getting at
is, could these be used

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:39.650
to transport something useful,
not just a pellet, but a drug

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:43.223
or medicine to the precise
location of disease?

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:47.850
And so, the advantage of using
a human patient's own cells

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:51.530
is that the immune response
system wouldn't flush it out,

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:55.110
unlike other micro
robotic delivery systems

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:57.163
made of metal, for example.

00:08:02.731 --> 00:08:03.570
If the production

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:06.130
of xenobots, which exists right now,

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:08.440
could be scaled up hugely,

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:11.440
by automating them with 3D printers,

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:13.550
then they could be used to tackle issues

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:16.653
on a global scale, such as pollution.

00:08:17.570 --> 00:08:21.630
We could imagine without too
much further bioengineering

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:24.604
simply swarms of xenobots
that swirl around and clean,

00:08:24.604 --> 00:08:27.054
simply swarms of xenobots
that swirl around and clean,

00:08:27.054 --> 00:08:29.360
could be used in the environment

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:34.000
to clean up little
microscopic bits of plastic,

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:37.780
to aggregate them into
a larger ball of plastic

00:08:37.780 --> 00:08:39.350
that could be identified

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:42.730
and picked up by traditional
robots, boats, or drones,

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:45.173
and then taken to a recycling center.

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:51.150
Just wear and tear on
robots is awful difficult.

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:54.860
So, it's very easy to lose
functionality with small changes,

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:57.460
especially when you're
dealing with physical motors.

00:08:58.490 --> 00:09:02.250
It's fairly catastrophic when
there's any sort of damage.

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:04.980
But biological systems
for millions of years

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:09.340
have been optimized to resist
wear and tear and damage

00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:12.010
and certainly, amphibians
have many of the same pathways

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:13.287
that we humans do.

00:09:13.287 --> 00:09:15.180
So, leveraging that's been great,

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:18.380
it gives us the ability to have organisms

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:21.390
that when they're damaged,
can continue functioning

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:23.100
even with fairly catastrophic damage.

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:26.900
So you can largely bisect the xenobot

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:29.460
and it will zipper itself
back up in about five minutes

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:32.480
and continue on doing what it did before

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:33.840
and this is really amazing.

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:36.140
We haven't seen another
system that can do something

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:37.320
to that extreme.

00:09:39.329 --> 00:09:41.640
And Blackiston and
Kriegman believe it's not

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:44.180
just the xenobots that are revolutionary,

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:47.073
but also the supercomputer
that's used to create them.

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:49.790
One day, it could potentially

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:53.410
help humans redesign themselves.

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:55.990
We have supercomputers
that can run millions

00:09:55.990 --> 00:09:57.803
of simulations per minute

00:09:58.810 --> 00:10:01.450
and it also can be optimized
for whatever we would like.

00:10:01.450 --> 00:10:03.410
So as humans, we often have goals

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:06.020
that may be slightly different
than natural selection.

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:09.770
And we can now have the
computer set up evolution

00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:12.800
and sort of get through
this bottleneck much faster.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:15.313
So for example, for the heart,

00:10:15.313 --> 00:10:18.520
we're seeing that with the types of diets

00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:20.470
that humans eat now,

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:23.060
there may be some other
type of heart valve

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:25.040
or even some process in the body

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:28.910
or vasculature that's superior
to what we humans have

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:30.690
and the computer can likely hone in

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:33.086
on those types of changes very quickly.

00:10:35.030 --> 00:10:39.631
I think that the point of
the computer simulations

00:10:39.631 --> 00:10:40.464
I think that the point of
the computer simulations

00:10:40.464 --> 00:10:43.125
isn't just to speed up evolution.

00:10:43.125 --> 00:10:44.741
It's that human designers
have really strong biases

00:10:44.741 --> 00:10:47.790
It's that human designers
have really strong biases

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:50.470
in the kinds of artifacts that we make.

00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:53.760
So, when a human goes to design a robot,

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:58.600
they inevitably will copy
some familiar object.

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:02.300
The robot will look like a
dog or a human or a plant

00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:03.450
that they've seen before,

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:06.250
some kinds of shapes that
are familiar to them.

00:11:06.250 --> 00:11:09.560
Now, the computer
doesn't share our biases.

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:12.368
It might have it's own biases in it.

00:11:12.368 --> 00:11:13.759
But it will come up with
designs that look nothing

00:11:13.759 --> 00:11:16.242
But it will come up with
designs that look nothing

00:11:16.242 --> 00:11:20.200
like any organism or thing
that we've seen in the world

00:11:23.288 --> 00:11:25.718
And then it's a matter of
tackling all the big ethical

00:11:25.718 --> 00:11:28.965
and societal questions about
should those be implemented?

00:11:28.965 --> 00:11:30.258
and societal questions about
should those be implemented?

00:11:30.258 --> 00:11:32.136
How much control should we have

00:11:32.136 --> 00:11:36.030
over our bodies and the design
of humans or other organisms?

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:38.840
These are the same things
that the plant scientists

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:40.040
have been grappling with, right?

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:42.750
It's can you make a bigger tomato?

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:44.263
Can you make a bigger apple? Should you?

00:11:44.263 --> 00:11:46.210
Should you make it pesticide resistant?

00:11:46.210 --> 00:11:48.150
And I think what we're going
see are those questions

00:11:48.150 --> 00:11:50.600
moving into humans very soon.

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:53.409
We're starting small,
we're using xenobots,

00:11:53.409 --> 00:11:54.550
but you can extrapolate this on up.

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:59.330
So, if a computer is so good
at designing a microorganism,

00:11:59.330 --> 00:12:01.630
could the computer design a better horse?

00:12:01.630 --> 00:12:03.980
Could the computer design a better cat?

00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:06.560
Could this computer make
something that's more efficient,

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:08.470
a better heart for humans,

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:10.110
if given the right amount of information

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:12.350
and the right amount
of synthetic evolution?

00:12:12.350 --> 00:12:13.720
And so, I think there's a real push

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:18.010
to understand how can AI be
harnessed to really capitalize

00:12:18.010 --> 00:12:21.533
and even push where evolution
has gotten us to this point.

